9359	Configure the methods used to decide which language will be used to display text on your site.		10.5.8
9360	What is a language detection method?		10.5.8
9361	A language detection method is a way for your site to decide what language should be used to display text. This decision is made by evaluating a series of detection methods for languages; the first detection method that gets a result will determine which language is used. If you have more than one language on your site, you can configure the detection methods that are used and the order they are evaluated, for both user interface text and content text. Configuration text always uses the interface text method, and most sites use the same detection methods for all types of text.		10.5.8
9362	What language detection methods are available?		10.5.8
9363	The available detection methods depend on what modules you have installed. Some of the most commonly-used language detection methods are:		10.5.8
9364	Use the language from the URL, which can either be in a path prefix like <em>example.com/fr/page_path</em> for French (fr) language, or in a domain like <em>fr.example.com/page_path</em>.		10.5.8
9365	For logged in users, use the language they have set in their profiles.		10.5.8
9366	Use the language preferences from the user's browser.		10.5.8
9367	Use the language most recently selected from a <em>Language switcher</em> block.		10.5.8
9368	Use the language from a session parameter or a request parameter; for example, adding <em>?language=fr</em> to the end of the URL.		10.5.8
9369	Check the boxes to enable the desired language detection methods, and uncheck boxes for the methods you do not want to use.		10.5.8
9370	Drag the methods to change their order, if desired.		10.5.8
9371	Click <em>Save settings</em>.		10.5.8
9372	Click <em>Configure</em> on each enabled detection method and verify that its configuration is correct (or update the configuration if not).		10.5.8
